# {{ project_name }}

A cross-platform C++{{ cpp_standard }} library using CMake, vcpkg, and GoogleTest.

## Reproducible setup and validation

From a fresh checkout:

```bash
python scripts/bootstrap.py
python scripts/validate.py
```

Requires Python 3.12+, Git and a platform C++17 toolchain (GCC/Clang on Linux; Visual Studio 2022 C++ tools and a Windows SDK on Windows). Linux also needs curl, zip, unzip and tar. Bootstrap installs pinned tools and the declared vcpkg baseline in `.dev-tools`. Validation configures, builds the static/shared libraries and example, runs CTest, and validates the installed C++ port with an independent consumer.

The scripts supply tool paths automatically, including Ninja inside vcpkg compiler detection. Compiler tracking remains enabled. No shell activation or manual path setup is required.

## Build presets

Validation uses `windows-vcpkg` / `windows-release` / `windows-release-tests` on Windows and `linux-vcpkg-release` / `linux-release` / `linux-release-tests` on Linux. Windows uses Visual Studio 2022; Linux uses Ninja. `CMakePresets.json` also contains Debug presets. Keep preset changes aligned with `scripts/validate.py`.

## Dependencies and overlay ports

`vcpkg.json` declares project dependencies. The generated `vcpkg-configuration.json` uses Microsoft vcpkg as the only package registry and declares the repository-local `vcpkg-ports` directory as an overlay.

The `gtest` dependency is resolved from `vcpkg-ports/gtest` before registry lookup. This local port preserves the official GoogleTest build and standard `GTest::*` CMake targets, but configures pkg-config metadata with `SKIP_CHECK`, so vcpkg does not acquire or execute `pkgconf`.

No personal vcpkg registry is required. When the custom recipe changes, update the files under `vcpkg-ports/gtest` and validate the project on Windows and Linux. All other package names continue to resolve from the Microsoft default registry.

## Continuous integration

Pushing this project to GitHub automatically runs `.github/workflows/build-and-test.yml` on Windows and Linux. Pull requests and manual runs also trigger it. The workflow invokes `python scripts/bootstrap.py` and `python scripts/validate.py`; those scripts build the libraries, examples and tests, check that `pkgconf` was neither installed nor acquired, and run CTest.

To add another dependency:

```bash
vcpkg add port <package-name>
```

The package resolves from Microsoft vcpkg unless a same-named local port exists under `vcpkg-ports`.

## Installable C++ library

The regular presets build the independent `{{ project_slug }}` static target and `{{ project_slug }}_shared` shared target, plus examples/tests. Public headers install under `include/{{ project_slug }}/`, compiled libraries under `lib/` (Windows DLLs under `bin/`), and CMake config/version/targets under `lib/cmake/{{ project_slug }}/`. Consumers use `find_package({{ project_slug }} CONFIG REQUIRED)` and `{{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }}`. The package version comes from the numeric `version` Copier answer; version compatibility is within the same major version. Public include paths distinguish the build tree from the install tree, and the target propagates C++{{ cpp_standard }}.

The local port is `vcpkg-ports/{{ vcpkg_port_name }}`. Its `vcpkg.json` describes the library to install and depends only on vcpkg CMake helpers. The root `vcpkg.json` continues to describe development dependencies such as GoogleTest; it is not the port manifest. Keep the port inside this source checkout: it locates the library using `../..`, builds the current working tree, and installs no tests or examples. Future production dependencies must also be declared in the port and its exported config where appropriate.

Normal CMake builds default `BUILD_STATIC_LIBRARY=ON` and `BUILD_SHARED_LIBRARY=ON`; a direct install exports `{{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }}` and `{{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }}_shared`. The port reads `VCPKG_LIBRARY_LINKAGE` and enables only the matching target. Static-only and shared-only ports both export `{{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }}`; there is no duplicate/conflicting second target in a port. `x64-linux` selects static, while `x64-windows` selects shared. Windows uses `WINDOWS_EXPORT_ALL_SYMBOLS` for the current function API. Future global data or some virtual-class interfaces require explicit DLL import/export annotations.

To test packaging alone after bootstrap:

```bash
python scripts/validate_vcpkg.py
```

For a direct CMake install, configure with `BUILD_TESTING=OFF` and `BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF`, build, then run `cmake --install <build-directory> --config Release --prefix <install-directory>`. The vcpkg recipe performs this for both Debug and Release and combines their imported targets.

## Independent downstream consumer

A C++ consumer needs CMake, vcpkg and the platform compiler. Python, Poetry and pybind11 are not consumer dependencies. Place the consumer beside the generated project, rather than inside its source/build tree. Create `consumer/vcpkg.json`:

```json
{ "dependencies": ["{{ vcpkg_port_name }}"] }
```

Create `consumer/vcpkg-configuration.json`, with the generated project's pinned baseline and a relative overlay path (replace `my-project` with this checkout's directory):

```json
{
  "default-registry": {
    "kind": "git",
    "baseline": "00807e89724847a866033a2f042c38fafae3c534",
    "repository": "https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg"
  },
  "overlay-ports": ["../my-project/vcpkg-ports"]
}
```

Create `consumer/CMakeLists.txt`:

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.25)
project(consumer LANGUAGES CXX)
find_package({{ project_slug }} CONFIG REQUIRED)
add_executable(consumer main.cpp)
target_link_libraries(consumer PRIVATE {{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }})
```

Create `consumer/main.cpp`:

```cpp
#include <{{ project_slug }}/example.h>
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    const auto message = HelloWorld();
    std::cout << message << std::endl;
    return message == "Hello, World!" ? 0 : 1;
}
```

Linux, from `consumer/`, with `$VCPKG_ROOT` pointing to a bootstrapped checkout:

```bash
"$VCPKG_ROOT/vcpkg" install --triplet x64-linux --binarysource=clear
cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE="$VCPKG_ROOT/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake" -DVCPKG_{{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }}_TRIPLET=x64-linux -DVCPKG_INSTALLED_DIR="$PWD/vcpkg_installed" -DVCPKG_MANIFEST_INSTALL=OFF
cmake --build build
./build/consumer
```

Windows PowerShell, from `consumer/`, with `$env:VCPKG_ROOT` set:

```powershell
& "$env:VCPKG_ROOT/vcpkg.exe" install --triplet x64-windows --binarysource=clear
cmake -S . -B build -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A x64 "-DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$env:VCPKG_ROOT/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake" -DVCPKG_{{ project_slug }}::{{ project_slug }}_TRIPLET=x64-windows "-DVCPKG_INSTALLED_DIR=$PWD/vcpkg_installed" -DVCPKG_MANIFEST_INSTALL=OFF
cmake --build build --config Release
./build/Release/consumer.exe
```

For Windows Debug, use `--config Debug` and `./build/Debug/consumer.exe`. To select a static Windows library with dynamic CRT, replace `x64-windows` with `x64-windows-static-md` in both commands and use a new build/install directory. Linux shared linkage is also covered using an isolated overlay triplet that includes `x64-linux.cmake` and sets `VCPKG_LIBRARY_LINKAGE dynamic`.

Local source changes do not change vcpkg's port recipe hash. Use a fresh consumer install directory, or remove the already installed port before reinstalling; keep `--binarysource=clear` to force compilation from the working tree. Moving this port away from its source checkout is unsupported. CI covers Linux x64 and Windows x64 with Visual Studio 2022; other platforms/toolchains and cross compilation are not validated.

## Template updates

Commit `.copier-answers.yml` and project changes before `copier update --vcs-ref <template-tag>`. Template Git tags version updates independently of your project's version.
